&#8220;My newest fantasy is about feeling good in my body and enjoying my life,&#8221; she told Sports Illustrated . &#8220;I worked so hard that I destroyed my nervous system. I have such bad anxiety that now I&#8217;m working on reversing those things because it’s not conducive to my career. It doesn’t help &#8230; to be an artist of this caliber and to have anxiety and to have fear. &#8230; There’s no place for that. I really am just seeking joy.&#8221;

This grounded, restorative outlook previews Lizzo&#8217;s exciting upcoming slate of projects. The Grammy-winning singer-rapper-songwriter hasn&#8217;t been on the silver screen in an acting role since 2019&#8217;s acclaimed Hustlers , but she&#8217;ll soon return in the starring role of a forthcoming Sister Rosetta Tharpe biopic.

Confirming that the film was greenlit and in development, Lizzo revealed, &#8220;We’re in the process of writing the script. There’s a lot of research that has gone into the biopic, and I want to be really careful and precious with the script because I think it’s really important to show a Black woman at that time in a different light. &#8230; I want her biopic to represent her as the superstar that she was.&#8221;

View this post on Instagram

In addition to her acting career, Lizzo is also bringing her talents to the world of children&#8217;s books. This fall (Sept. 8), she&#8217;ll release Lil Lizzo Meets Sasha B. Flootin&#8217;, a Mark Pett-illustrated picture book that follows &#8220;a girl [who] finds her voice with the help of a smart and sassy flute.&#8221; &#8220;I want to&#8230; give people backstory on my relationship,&#8221; she said. &#8220;I’m super adamant about young people playing instruments again. It used to be mandatory, and I don’t know if it’s mandatory anymore, and it scares me.&#8221;

It&#8217;s been a busy month for Lizzo, most of which she&#8217;s spent as a guest professor at Kai Cenat&#8217;s Streamer University, where she notably gave streaming star and Grammy-nominated artist Plaqueboymax helpful lessons in self-love after fellow &#8220;students&#8221; like online personality Ojaysuave harassed him and encouraged homophobia-laced speculation about his sexuality.

Last month (June 5), Lizzo&#8217;s Bitch became her first project since 2015&#8217;s Big Grrrl Small World to miss the Billboard 200 completely. Led by the singles &#8220;Don&#8217;t Make Me Love U&#8221; and the Meredith Brooks-nodding title track, the LP managed to reach No. 15 on Indie Store Album Sales and No. 46 on Top Album Sales.
